# Target C-II — shallow outer teeth force linear inward penetration

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Repository base:** `783e2e9711125a4c0028fea2f12c9ebcc42ae699`  
**Status:** proved proof-only geometric consequence; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** Type-II two-edge tooth branch; numerical corollary uses current AG42/§171 low-`t` floor  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The outer-tooth/long-side dichotomy leaves a concrete tooth branch.  This note turns a
large two-edge-tooth population plus small outer detour into a linear family of
distinct, strongly inward unit contacts.

Let a two-edge tooth have loaded base endpoints `a,b`, unloaded apex `x`, and nonunit
base length

\[
s=|a-b|\in(1,2].
\]

Both `ax` and `xb` are unit contacts.  Audited Section 126 identifies these teeth with
actual augmented triangular faces.

## 1. Most teeth are shallow when detour is small

Each two-edge tooth contributes exactly

\[
2-s
\]

to the metric outer detour

\[
d_{out}=b-\operatorname{per}(F).
\]

Fix `eta>0` and call a tooth `eta`-shallow if

\[
s\ge2-\eta.
\]

If `N_2` is the total tooth count and `N_{sh}(eta)` the shallow count, then Markov's
inequality applied to the exact detour sum gives

\[
\boxed{
N_{sh}(\eta)
\ge N_2-{d_{out}\over\eta}.
}
\tag{ST.1}
\]

No area estimate is used.

## 2. One apex supports at most two sufficiently shallow teeth

Set

\[
\alpha_\eta:=\arccos(1-\eta/2).
\]

For an `eta`-shallow tooth, its two loaded endpoints lie on the unit circle centered
at `x` and their smaller angular separation `gamma` satisfies

\[
\gamma\ge\pi-2\alpha_\eta.
\tag{ST.2}
\]

All contact neighbors of `x` are source points at mutual distance at least one, so
their direction angles on the unit circle are pairwise separated by at least `pi/3`.
For one endpoint of a shallow tooth, every possible partner lies in the arc of radius
`2 alpha_eta` about its antipodal direction.  The total width of that arc is
`4 alpha_eta`.

If

\[
\boxed{\alpha_\eta<\pi/12,}
\tag{ST.3}
\]

then this partner arc has width less than `pi/3`, so it contains at most one other
contact neighbor.  Therefore the endpoint pairs belonging to `eta`-shallow teeth form
a matching in the contact-neighbor set of `x`.

Canonical C26 gives

\[
3\le\deg_C(x)\le5
\]

for every unloaded contact-core vertex.  Hence a matching among the neighbors has at
most two edges:

\[
\boxed{
\text{one unloaded apex supports at most two `eta`-shallow teeth.}
}
\tag{ST.4}
\]

Condition (ST.3) is equivalent to

\[
\eta<2(1-\cos(\pi/12))
=0.06814834742186\ldots .
\]

## 3. Every shallow apex has a strongly inward extra contact

Choose one shallow tooth `a-x-b` at a distinct apex `x`.  Since `deg_C(x)>=3`, choose
a contact neighbor `w` distinct from `a,b`.

The apex belongs to an unloaded component `I_r`.  Harmonic trapping places every
unloaded vertex of that component inside its attachment polygon `P_r subset F`; every
loaded vertex is also in `F`.  Thus

\[
w\in F.
\tag{ST.5}
\]

Choose coordinates with

\[
a=(-\cos\alpha,0),\qquad
b=(\cos\alpha,0),\qquad
x=(0,\sin\alpha),
\]

where

\[
0<\alpha\le\alpha_\eta,
\]

and with `F` in the half-plane `y>=0`.  Write

\[
w-x=(X,Y),\qquad X^2+Y^2=1.
\]

Triangle-freeness gives

\[
|w-a|>1,\qquad |w-b|>1,
\]

because equality would create a unit equilateral triangle using the contact `xw` and
one tooth side.  Expanding the two inequalities gives

\[
\cos\alpha\,|X|-\sin\alpha\,Y<1/2.
\tag{ST.6}
\]

Write `Y=sin beta`, `|X|=cos beta` with `beta in[-pi/2,pi/2]`.  Since `w in F`,

\[
\sin\alpha+\sin\beta\ge0,
\]

hence `beta>=-alpha`.  Equation (ST.6) gives

\[
\cos(\alpha+\beta)<1/2.
\]

Under (ST.3), `0<=alpha+beta<2pi/3`, so

\[
\beta>\pi/3-\alpha.
\]

Therefore every such extra contact has inward normal component

\[
\boxed{
Y>\sin(\pi/3-\alpha)
\ge Y_\eta:=\sin(\pi/3-\alpha_\eta).
}
\tag{ST.7}
\]

The explicit algebraic form is

\[
Y_\eta
={\sqrt3\over2}(1-\eta/2)
-{1\over2}\sqrt{\eta-\eta^2/4}.
\tag{ST.8}
\]

## 4. Distinct penetration-edge count

By (ST.4), `N_sh(eta)` shallow teeth use at least

\[
{N_{sh}(\eta)\over2}
\]

distinct apices.  At each distinct apex choose one shallow tooth and one extra contact
`xw` as above.  The same undirected contact edge can be selected by at most its two
endpoints.  Hence the number `E_pen(eta)` of distinct selected penetration contacts
satisfies

\[
\boxed{
E_{pen}(\eta)
\ge {N_{sh}(\eta)\over4}
\ge {1\over4}\left(N_2-{d_{out}\over\eta}\right).
}
\tag{ST.9}
\]

Every selected edge has inward normal component greater than `Y_eta` relative to the
base of its chosen shallow tooth.

This conclusion keeps physical edges distinct; it is not a KKT-weight count.

## 5. Current low-`t` numerical tooth branch

At the present proof-only floor

\[
t_{42}=C_{146}+0.0013751,
\]

the low-`t` squeeze gives

\[
{d_{out}\over D}\le0.002883975+o(1).
\]

The outer-tooth/long-side dichotomy gives the tooth alternative

\[
{N_2\over D}\ge
0.4363893718701161\ldots-o(1).
\tag{ST.10}
\]

Choose

\[
\eta=0.02.
\]

Then `alpha_eta=arccos(0.99)<pi/12`, so (ST.4) applies.  Equation (ST.1) gives

\[
\boxed{
{N_{sh}(0.02)\over D}
\ge0.2921906218701161\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{ST.11}
\]

Thus there are at least

\[
\boxed{
0.1460953109350580\ldots D-o(D)
}
\tag{ST.12}
\]

distinct shallow-tooth apices, and at least

\[
\boxed{
{E_{pen}(0.02)\over D}
\ge0.0730476554675290\ldots-o(1)
}
\tag{ST.13}
\]

distinct inward penetration contacts.

For `eta=0.02`, (ST.8) gives

\[
\boxed{
Y_{0.02}=0.7868314698482648\ldots .
}
\tag{ST.14}
\]

So every selected penetration edge advances at least `0.7868...` in the inward normal
direction of one near-diameter-two loaded-hull tooth base.

## 6. Frontier effect

This is a genuine geometric strengthening of the tooth branch:

```text
low t + tooth alternative
 -> >0.29219 D shallow actual outer teeth
 -> >0.14609 D distinct unloaded shallow apices
 -> >0.07304 D distinct inward unit contacts
 -> each has normal penetration >0.7868.
```

The theorem does not use or produce a positive lower bound on the KKT weight of those
contacts.  That is important: the known near-chain regressions can hide transverse
geometry behind small equilibrium weights.

The next closing target is therefore to inject this **unweighted physical penetration
population** into one of the already weight-free structures: the coherent rhombic
phase boundary, the harmonic-cell attachment incidence ledger, or a source-preserving
multi-tooth shear.  A purely weighted stress summation can still lose the result.

The long-side alternative remains separate and open.

## 7. Reproducibility

Run

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_shallow_tooth_penetration.py
```

for the constant checks.
